#use(std/code)

#section{Document Sets (##toctree)}::

#code{#use(std/toctree)} provides #code{#toctree} — the sphinx-style
"manifest in the language". The index document #emph{is} the manifest: its
#code{#toctree} lists child documents (raw body, one path per non-blank
line, relative to the current file), and the doc-set graph is discovered by
evaluating the root. This very how-to is built that way — see
#code{index.yamd}:

#code(lang: "plain")
	#use(std/toctree)

	#section{My Book}::

	#toctree(numbered: #t depth: 2)
		intro.yamd
		guide/setup.yamd

There is no live demo on this page: a document may appear in exactly one
toctree, and every page here is already claimed by the index. The index's
own nav #emph{is} the live demo

Options:

#list
	#item
		#code{depth:} (positive integer) bounds how deep the #strong{nav} recurses; it is ignored when inlining in
		single-file mode (content is never truncated) and does not bound #code{#toc}
	#item
		#code{numbered: #t} adds hierarchical section numbers ("1", "1.1", "2") to the children's headings, navs and
		#code{#toc} entries, in document order, identically in both build modes. Nested toctrees inherit the scheme; sibling
		numbered toctrees each restart at "1"
	#item
		#code{hidden: #t} registers the children (they build, refs resolve, pages get prev/next/up) but emits no nav at the
		call site — multi-file only; single-file inlines the content regardless

Build modes (see the CLI page): #strong{single} inlines each child at its
marker with headings shifted by the call site's depth; #strong{multi} renders
a nav of links and one page per document, rewriting cross-page
#code{#anchor} links

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		A toctree child evaluates with #code{'header-depth} reset to 0 — it is a standalone document whose top section is
		h1 (unlike an #code{#include}d fragment, which inherits depth)
	#item
		A document may appear in exactly one toctree; duplicates, cycles and missing files are explicit errors. File
		identity is symlink-resolved, so aliased spellings unify
	#item
		Rendering a toctree document without the builder (plain #code{yamd-file->html}) fails loudly on the internal marker
		— use the CLI, which always routes through the builder
	#item
		A numbered document with no heading of its own gets no number; if it also has toctree children, that is an explicit
		error
	#item: An empty toctree body is an error
